 </tbody></table></div>
-<p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;max-width:720px;margin-top:.9rem">Price ranges are the working ranges on each area page and move with the market. Longs has no area page yet; prices there vary widely with land. If you plan to rent the home out, the town ordinance and the HOA covenants for the address govern, and we will pull both. The feature column is what each area is physically known for, not a judgement about it. The town of Pawleys Island is entirely in Georgetown County. A Murrells Inlet mailing address can be in Horry County or in Georgetown County, which changes which county rules apply. The rules on the sand itself, dogs, tents and parking, differ by town as well, on our <a href="/buyers/relocating/beaches/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">beach rules page</a>. For the hospital nearest any address, see the <a href="/buyers/relocating/healthcare/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">healthcare page</a>.</p>
+<p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;max-width:720px;margin-top:.9rem">Price ranges are the working ranges on each area page and move with the market. Longs has no area page yet; prices there vary widely with land. Our page on the <a href="/buyers/affordable-places-to-live/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">most affordable places to buy a home near Myrtle Beach</a> compares Longs with Loris, Aynor, Conway and Georgetown. If you plan to rent the home out, the town ordinance and the HOA covenants for the address govern, and we will pull both. The feature column is what each area is physically known for, not a judgement about it. The town of Pawleys Island is entirely in Georgetown County. A Murrells Inlet mailing address can be in Horry County or in Georgetown County, which changes which county rules apply. The rules on the sand itself, dogs, tents and parking, differ by town as well, on our <a href="/buyers/relocating/beaches/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">beach rules page</a>. For the hospital nearest any address, see the <a href="/buyers/relocating/healthcare/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">healthcare page</a>.</p>
 <div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Not sure which of these your budget reaches?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Shortlist my towns</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">City or county</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Incorporated or unincorporated: what changes</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Incorporated means the address sits inside a city or town that runs its own services and writes its own rules. Unincorporated means the county does both. It does not show up in a listing, and it changes four bills.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Sales tax</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Nine percent inside the City of Myrtle Beach, eight percent in the county. Groceries are untaxed either way.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Trash</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Curbside collection is a city service. Outside city limits you arrange it yourself, and the options and costs are on the <a href="/buyers/relocating/moving-checklist/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">new resident checklist</a>.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Beach parking</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The free resident parking decal is issued by the city to city residents. Living two streets outside the line means paying at the meters.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">The HOA is separate from all of it</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">City limits decide the four bills above. The covenants on your deed decide how you live in the house, and they apply whether you are inside a town or not. Most newer neighborhoods here have an association; older streets and rural land often do not. Before you offer, read what the covenants say about boats, campers, work vehicles, fences, sheds, pools, exterior paint and pets, and read what the dues cover. We pull the <a href="/hoa/documents/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">documents</a> for any address, and the <a href="/hoa/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">HOA guides</a> explain <a href="/hoa/dues-increases/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">dues increases</a>, <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">special assessments</a> and <a href="/hoa/violations-and-fines/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">violations and fines</a> before you are living with them.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Golf carts and rentals</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Each town writes its own golf cart rules, and the county writes them for unincorporated areas, so a cart that is street-legal in one neighborhood may not be in the next. Renting your house out works the same way: the town or county decides, and your HOA can say no on top of that. Check the rules for the specific address, not the town name.</p></div></section>
